+\paragraph{General finite-field Frobenius comparison (Block 27).}
+Let $L/\Q$ now be any finite normal number field, with a chosen embedding
+$j:L\hookrightarrow\overline{\Q}$. Write $\mathcal A$ for the integral
+closure of $\mathbb Z$ in $\overline{\Q}$. The embedding restricts to an
+injective map $\mathcal O_L\to\mathcal A$ which intertwines the actual
+Galois actions. If $\mathfrak Q\subset\mathcal A$ is maximal above $(p)$,
+its contraction $\mathfrak P\subset\mathcal O_L$ is a nonzero maximal
+ideal above $(p)$, and the induced residue map is injective.
+
+Restriction carries the actual decomposition group at $\mathfrak Q$ into
+that at $\mathfrak P$, carries ideal inertia into ideal inertia, and
+preserves the arithmetic $p$-power residue action. Thus the absolute
+Frobenius constructed in Block 26 gives a simultaneous finite Frobenius
+at the contracted prime. This assertion applies to arbitrary finite normal
+number fields and does not require a cyclotomic generator.
+
+The exceptional primes are controlled by the actual field discriminant:
+\[
+ S_L=\{p\text{ rational prime}:p\mid\operatorname{disc}(L)\}.
+\]
+This set is finite. For $p\notin S_L$, every prime of $\mathcal O_L$
+above $(p)$ is unramified. Lean proves that the actual inertia cardinality
+is the ramification index. It follows that inertia is trivial and the
+arithmetic Frobenius at a fixed finite prime is unique as a field
+automorphism. Neither trivial inertia nor uniqueness is assumed in place
+of the arithmetic unramifiedness condition.
+
+The choice of absolute prime and Frobenius lift is also controlled. Let
+$(\mathfrak Q_u,\gamma_u)$ and $(\mathfrak Q_v,\gamma_v)$ be any two
+actual arithmetic witnesses at the same rational prime. Transitivity on
+primes of $\mathcal A$ above $(p)$ gives $a\in G_{\Q}$ with
+\[
+ \mathfrak Q_v=a\mathfrak Q_u,\qquad
+ \gamma_v^{-1}a\gamma_u a^{-1}\in I_{\mathfrak Q_v}.
+\]
+Consequently, any representation trivial on $I_{\mathfrak Q_v}$ has the
+same trace on the two witnesses. For representations factoring through
+$L$, triviality on this actual absolute inertia is derived whenever
+$p\notin S_L$. The restrictions of all absolute witnesses at such a prime
+therefore form a single conjugacy class. This conclusion requires only
+the indicated finite factorization and the discriminant exclusion.
+
+There is a final compatibility between embeddings. For a normal subfield
+$L\subset\overline{\Q}$, restriction defined through a chosen embedding
+is conjugate to canonical restriction by one fixed automorphism of $L$.
+This identifies their conjugacy classes and lets the arithmetic comparison
+apply directly to the fixed fields of open normal subgroups of $G_{\Q}$.
+Those fixed fields are proved to be finite Galois number fields, with the
+original open normal subgroup as the kernel of restriction.
+
+The remaining prime-existence obligation now has a precise formulation.
+For every finite Galois subfield $L$, every finite set $B$ of rational
+primes, and every $\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(L/\Q)$, require a prime
+$p\notin B\cup S_L$ and an actual absolute arithmetic witness $\gamma_p$
+such that
+\[
+ q_L(\gamma_p)\sim\sigma.
+\]
+Lean proves that this assertion is equivalent to the cofinite quotient
+coverage required by the general separator, for any fixed choice of actual
+Frobenius witnesses. The equivalence derives independence from the witness
+choices; it does not supply the displayed prime-existence assertion.
+Arbitrary finite exclusions absorb $S_L$, and the resulting conditional
+density theorem preserves the separate good-prime sets of coefficient
+members. General prime coverage remains unproved, so Claim 9.1 remains
+partial. The source ledger is still seventeen checked and three partial.
+This new arithmetic interface has base $\Q$. For the source's general-base
+instances involving $G_{F,S}$, the analogous actual-prime and norm-power
+Frobenius attachment and coverage over $F$, or a proved transfer to the
+rational-base result, also remain necessary.
 
